WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.821
Idea. Say you save one hundred pounds, and the bank pays two per cent interest a year. How much do you earn? You need two per cent of one hundred pounds.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:19.955
The rule: divide the rate by one hundred, then multiply by the amount. Two divided by one hundred, times one hundred pounds, is two pounds.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:36.005
You use this in three ways. Interest is a percentage of savings, added on. Income tax is a percentage of the taxed part of income, taken off. A tariff, a tax on imports, is a percentage of the import price, added on.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:45.340
Method. Step one, find the base the rate applies to: the savings, only the taxed part of income, or the import price.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:56.350
Step two, work out the amount: rate over one hundred, times the base. For example, a ten per cent tariff on a two hundred dollar import is twenty dollars.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:05.541
Step three, if a final figure is wanted, add or subtract. Step four, write the working, and give the answer with its currency.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:22.173
Worked example. Here is a real question from November 2020. George earns twenty-one thousand euros as a manager in Cyprus, but only pays tax on one thousand five hundred euros. Calculate his net pay. Two marks.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:32.711
Find the base. The first nineteen thousand five hundred euros is taxed at zero. Only the last one thousand five hundred is taxed, at twenty per cent.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:50.837
Tax is twenty over one hundred, times one thousand five hundred euros, which is three hundred euros. Net pay is twenty-one thousand minus three hundred: twenty thousand seven hundred euros. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:02:02.535
Common mistake. A common mistake is taxing the whole salary. That gives four thousand two hundred euros of tax, which is wrong. Only the taxed band counts.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:10.629
Another is stopping at the tax, when the question asks for net pay. Three hundred euros is the tax, not the answer.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:32.547
Your turn. Now you try. Amira imports a bicycle for two hundred and forty dollars, and a fifteen per cent tariff is charged on the import price. Calculate the price including the tariff. Then, Ben saves eight hundred dollars at three per cent a year. Calculate his interest for one year. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:50.559
Answer. Part a. The tariff is fifteen over one hundred, times two hundred and forty dollars: thirty-six dollars. Add it on: two hundred and seventy-six dollars. Part b. Three over one hundred, times eight hundred dollars, is twenty-four dollars.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:59.173
See the bicycle price grow, from two hundred and forty dollars to two hundred and seventy-six dollars, once the tariff is added.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:09.859
Remember: find the base, work out rate over one hundred times the base, then add or subtract, and show the currency. Now try the practice questions.
